\begin{proof}
Let $P$ denote the set of condensation points of $E$, where $x$ is a condensation point if every neighborhood of $x$ meets $E$ in an uncountable set.

First, $P$ is closed. If $x\notin P$, some open neighborhood $U$ of $x$ satisfies $E\cap U$ countable. Choose an open neighborhood $V$ of $x$ with $V\subset U$. For every $y\in V$, the neighborhood $U$ of $y$ witnesses that $y\notin P$. Hence $V\subseteq P^c$, so $P^c$ is open.

It remains to show that $P$ has no isolated points. Fix $x\in P$ and $r>0$. Suppose, toward a contradiction, that $x$ is the only point of $P$ in $B(x,r)$. Every $y\in B(x,r)\setminus\{x\}$ is then not a condensation point, so it has an open neighborhood $U_y$ such that $E\cap U_y$ is countable. Since $\mathbb R^k$ is second countable, the open cover $\{U_y:y\in B(x,r)\setminus\{x\}\}$ has a countable subcover. Therefore $E\cap(B(x,r)\setminus\{x\})$ is countable. Adding the possible point $x$ shows that $E\cap B(x,r)$ is countable, contradicting $x\in P$.

Thus every neighborhood of each point of $P$ contains another point of $P$. Since $P$ is closed and has no isolated points, $P$ is perfect.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Call $x\in\mathbb R^k$ a condensation point of $E$ if every open neighborhood of $x$ has uncountable intersection with $E$, and let $P$ be the set of all such points.

We first prove that $P$ is closed. If $x\notin P$, there is an open set $U\ni x$ such that $E\cap U$ is countable. Choose an open set $V$ with $x\in V\subset U$. For every $y\in V$, the same set $U$ is a neighborhood of $y$ whose intersection with $E$ is countable; hence $y\notin P$. Thus $V\subset P^c$, and $P^c$ is open.

Now let $x\in P$ and let $r>0$. If $x$ were isolated in $P$, then every point of $B(x,r)\setminus\{x\}$ would fail to be a condensation point. For each such point $y$, choose an open neighborhood $U_y$ with $E\cap U_y$ countable. The space $\mathbb R^k$ is second countable, so this open cover of $B(x,r)\setminus\{x\}$ has a countable subcover. Consequently, $E\cap(B(x,r)\setminus\{x\})$ is countable, and hence $E\cap B(x,r)$ is countable, contradicting $x\in P$.

Therefore $P$ has no isolated points. Being closed and having no isolated points, $P$ is perfect.
\end{proof}
